<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><channel><title>Efficient-Llm</title><link>https://jaehun.me/en/categories/efficient-llm/</link><description>Technical notes by Jaehun Ryu, a deep learning compiler engineer, on LLM inference, KV caches, compilers, C++, AI papers, and open-source code.</description><language>en-US</language><atom:link href="https://jaehun.me/en/categories/efficient-llm/index.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><title>[Paper Review] Llama-Nemotron: Efficient Reasoning Models</title><link>https://jaehun.me/en/posts/paper-review-llama-nemotron-efficient-reasoning-models/</link><guid isPermaLink="true">https://jaehun.me/en/posts/paper-review-llama-nemotron-efficient-reasoning-models/</guid><pubDate>Tue, 29 Jul 2025 00:00:00 +0900</pubDate><description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;a&#10; href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.00949v4"target="_blank"&#10; class="inline-flex items-center gap-1"&#10; &gt;Paper Link&lt;svg class="h-3 w-3 flex-shrink-0" id="external-link" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24"&gt;&lt;path fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M15 3h6v6m-11 5L21 3m-3 10v6a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H5a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V8a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h6"/&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&#10; &lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;h2 id="hydragen-the-secret-weapon-for-decoding-large-batches-with-shared-prefixes-up-to-32-faster"&gt;Hydragen: The Secret Weapon for Decoding Large Batches with Shared Prefixes up to 32× Faster&lt;a href="#hydragen-the-secret-weapon-for-decoding-large-batches-with-shared-prefixes-up-to-32-faster" class="heading-anchor" aria-label="Link to this section"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;h2 id="tldr"&gt;TL;DR&lt;a href="#tldr" class="heading-anchor" aria-label="Link to this section"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;By decomposing the prefix and suffix using &lt;em&gt;softmax denominator rescaling&lt;/em&gt;, Hydragen accelerates long-context, large-batch decoding on &lt;strong&gt;Code-Llama-13B&lt;/strong&gt; by up to &lt;em&gt;32x&lt;/em&gt; compared to vLLM, all while guaranteeing bit-for-bit identical output.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item></channel></rss>